{
	"figures": [
		{
			"id": "thousand-lights",
			"kind": "scene",
			"publish": "lossless",
			"shows": "All thousand point lights, 40 by 25 on a 2 m grid, 0.4 m above a floor with the sun and sky off, in the Lighting view (every surface white, so only the light reaching it shows): the grid's four edges in the frame, each light's range of 1.2 m ending its round pool, which meets its row and column neighbours and leaves dark grey gaps only at the diagonals",
			"caption": "A thousand point lights, 40 by 25 and 2 m apart, 0.4 m above a floor, all in view and none dropped, with the sun and sky off. The Lighting debug view draws every surface plain white, so the brightness is the light reaching it alone (Measuring light teaches the views). The range is 1.2 m, shorter than the example's 1.5 m, so each pool ends about 1.1 m out on the floor. It meets its neighbours in its row and column and leaves dark gaps only between diagonal neighbours, so a pixel is reached by only the few lights above it. No point light reaches the grey gaps and margin: without a sky the renderer adds a faint constant ambient light to every surface, and the bloom spreads a glow from the pools.",
			"numbers": "The setup's steps: 40 lights per row (LightRow and 24 duplicates, 25 rows), x and z 2 m apart, y 0.4 m, PointLight Range 1.2 and Intensity 20; the Ground scaled to 84 by 54 m. Taken 2026-11-18: the capture's renderStats read lights 1000 (in view) and droppedLights 0 on the GPU-driven path; camera at (0, 69.3, 40) looking at the origin, fov 40, so the whole grid is in frame.",
			"alt": "A white floor seen at a slant from high above, on a black background. A regular grid of round, bright pools of light, 40 across and 25 deep, covers its whole rectangle. Each pool fades at its rim and touches the pools beside, before and behind it, leaving small dark grey gaps only between diagonal ones. A dark grey margin of bare floor, a little brighter near the pools, surrounds the grid, and the pools shrink toward the far edge.",
